    <p>Hi there,</p>

    <p>I’ve finished drafting the new section for the numeric analysis library. I wanted to double-check the MathML rendering for the <strong>Basler Problem</strong> before we push this to production. We need to make sure the summation limits align correctly across different clients.</p>

    <p>Specifically, this is the exact markup I’m planning to inject into the <a href="#">Infinite Series module</a>. It represents the value of the Riemann zeta function at $s = 2$.</p>

    <p>
        Here is the expression:
        <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
            <mrow>
                <munderover>
                    <mo>∑</mo>
                    <mrow>
                        <mi>n</mi>
                        <mo>=</mo>
                        <mn>1</mn>
                    </mrow>
                    <mi>∞</mi>
                </munderover>
                <mfrac>
                    <mn>1</mn>
                    <msup>
                        <mi>n</mi>
                        <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                </mfrac>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mfrac>
                    <msup>
                        <mi>π</mi>
                        <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                </mfrac>
            </mrow>
        </math>
    </p>

    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac><annotation
              encoding="TeX">\frac{1}{x}</annotation></semantics></math>

    <p>The convergence is quite slow, so in the implementation code (see the <code>zeta_calc.py</code> attachment I sent earlier), I actually used the Euler-Maclaurin formula to speed up the approximation.</p>

    <p>Could you verify if the $\pi$ symbol and the infinity sign ($\infty$) are rendering correctly on your end? If it looks good, I'll proceed with the merge request.</p>

    <p>Best regards,</p>

    <p><strong>The Dev Team</strong><br>
